A Formal Framework for Embedded Inference, Structural Ignorance, and Testable Error Floors

Author: ouadi Maakoul 

---

1. Introduction

A physical theory is always built from within. An observer that constructs a model of the universe is itself a subsystem of that universe, subject to the same dynamical laws and resource limitations. This self‑embedding imposes fundamental constraints on what can be known with certainty. While individual limitations—measurement noise, Gödel incompleteness, computational irreducibility, thermodynamic bounds—have been studied separately, their collective consequence has not been unified into a single conditional impossibility theorem. This chapter provides such a unification.

We model the universe as a deterministic, discrete‑time dynamical system $\mathcal{U}$, and the observer as a finite, embedded agent $O_t$ with a lossy observation channel $\Pi$, an internal model $M_t$, and bounded physical resources. We prove that under physically motivated constraints, the observer cannot achieve verifiable exact reconstruction of the generative dynamics if the universe is computationally irreducible. The result is not absolute; it applies only when the conjunction of constraints holds. We formalise the impossibility as the Observer‑Bounded Inference Principle.

To convert this limit into a measurable quantity, we introduce the time‑dependent relative compressibility error $\delta_t^*$, grounded in algorithmic information theory, and outline an in‑silico experimental protocol. The chapter thus provides both a rigorous theoretical statement and a falsifiable empirical conjecture.

