2. The Universe as a Recursive Deterministic System

2.1 Fundamental Definitions

Definition 2.1 (Universe state and generative function).
The universe at discrete time $t\in\mathbb{N}$ is described by a state $U_t \in \mathcal{U}$, where $\mathcal{U}$ is a (possibly infinite) set. The dynamics is given by a total, deterministic function $F:\mathcal{U}\to\mathcal{U}$ such that
U_{t+1} = F(U_t).


The entire trajectory is $U_t = F^{\,t}(U_0)$, where $F^{\,0}=\text{id}$ and $F^{\,t+1}=F\circ F^{\,t}$.

Axiom 2.1 (Determinism). $F$ is total; each state uniquely determines the next.

Axiom 2.2 (Operational closure).
$\mathcal{U}$ is the maximal set of events in the causal past and future light‑cone of the embedded observer $O_t$. There is no operational meaning to “outside” this set (background independence; cf. Smolin 2002, Shi 2026). This formalises the structural incompleteness of embedded observation.

Definition 2.2 (Computational irreducibility).
$F$ is computationally irreducible if there exists no computable function $g:\mathbb{N}\to\mathcal{U}$ with $g(t)=F^{\,t}(U_0)$ for all $t$ that has strictly lower time complexity than simulating $F$ step‑by‑step (Wolfram 2002). We denote the class of such generating functions by $\mathcal{I}$.

Axiom 2.3 (Richness / irreducible class).
We restrict our analysis to universes $F\in\mathcal{I}$ that are computationally irreducible (hence non‑trivial) and rich enough to support an embedded finite observer. Examples include Rule 110 (Cook 2004), Rule 54 (Boccara et al. 2005), and causal‑set automata. Fully integrable or compressible systems (e.g., additive Rule 90) are excluded; they either cannot host complex observers or make the reconstruction problem trivial.

2.2 Computational Irreducibility and Its Consequences

Lemma 2.1 (Irreducibility forbids speed‑up). If $F\in\mathcal{I}$, then any computable function that yields $F^{\,t}(U_0)$ for all $t$ must have time complexity at least $\Omega(t)$ in the worst case.

Proof. By definition of $\mathcal{I}$, no strictly faster program exists. $\square$

In practice, this means the only general way to know the state at time $t$ is to run the system for $t$ steps.

