3. The Embedded Observer

3.1 Observation Channel

Definition 3.1 (Embedded observer).
An observer $O_t \subset U_t$ is a subsystem. It accrues observation data via a measurement map
\mathcal{O}_{\le t} = \Pi_{\le t}(U_0,\dots,U_t).


The map $\Pi$ is the composition of three distinct loss mechanisms:

1. Spatial coarse‑graining $\Pi_{\text{res}}$: the observer has only a finite spatial receptive field (e.g., a contiguous patch of width $w$).
2. Physical noise $\Pi_{\text{phys}}$: any realistic measurement introduces stochastic perturbations (modeled as additive Gaussian noise with variance $\sigma^2$ in the classical limit).
3. Computational bandwidth $\Pi_{\text{comp}}$: the observer’s inference machine has bounded capacity (finite hidden units, bounded gradient updates per time step).

Thus $\Pi = \Pi_{\text{phys}} \circ \Pi_{\text{comp}} \circ \Pi_{\text{res}}$. In the quantum case $\Pi$ reduces to a completely positive trace‑preserving (CPTP) map; classically it is a stochastic Markov kernel. In either regime, $\Pi$ is non‑invertible—information is irreversibly lost.

Proposition 3.1 (Information loss). For any non‑invertible $\Pi$, the mutual information between the observation and the true state is strictly less than the entropy of the universe: $I(\Pi(U_t); U_t) < H(U_t)$.
Proof. For a quantum channel, the Stinespring dilation gives $I(\Pi(U);U) = H(U) - H(U|E)$ with $H(U|E)>0$. The classical case follows from the data‑processing inequality. $\square$

3.2 Internal Model and Physical Finiteness

Definition 3.2 (Internal model).
The observer constructs a model $M_t = G(\mathcal{O}_{\le t})$, where $G$ is the inference procedure (e.g., a recurrent neural network). The model $M_t:\mathcal{U}\to\mathcal{U}$ is an algorithmic hypothesis about $F$.

Axiom 3.1 (Observer finiteness).
Any physically realisable observer has:

· finite computational capacity (bounded FLOPs per time step),
· finite memory (bounded number of trainable parameters),
· finite free energy, which we model via an effective thermodynamic cost proxy applied to logically irreversible operations (see §4.4).

Following Khan (2025), the observer can be seen as a System Identification Algorithm (SIA) whose capacity to track the external system is bounded by its own Kolmogorov complexity.

3.3 The Good Regulator Theorem (Relaxed)

Theorem 3.2 (Good Regulator; Conant & Ashby 1970; Virgo et al. 2025).
Every good regulator of a system must contain a partial homomorphic image of that system. For an embedded agent, the model $M_t$ needs to reflect only enough structural features to enable local regulation and anticipation, not an exact global replica.

This relaxation is crucial: our impossibility theorem does not forbid adaptive behaviour, only the verifiable attainment of the exact rule.

