5. The Observer‑Bounded Inference Principle (Main Theorem)

5.1 Verifiable Exact Reconstruction Defined

Definition 5.1 (Verifiable exact reconstruction).
An observer achieves verifiable exact reconstruction of $F$ if it outputs:

· a model $M$ (a computable function $M:\mathcal{U}\to\mathcal{U}$),
· a finite internal proof $V$ in a formal system $T$ (where $T$ extends Peano arithmetic) that $\forall U\in\mathcal{U},\; M(U)=F(U)$.

$V$ is a certificate that $M$ is not just phenomenologically accurate on past data, but identically equal to the generative function.

5.2 The Conditional Impossibility Theorem

Theorem 5.2 (Observer‑Bounded Inference — conditional impossibility).
Let $\mathcal{U}$ be a computationally irreducible universe (Axiom 2.3) that supports a finite embedded observer $O_t$ with a non‑invertible observation channel $\Pi$ (Axiom 3.1, Theorem 4.1). Then $O_t$ cannot achieve verifiable exact reconstruction of $F$.

Formally:
O_t \subset \mathcal{U} \;\Longrightarrow\; \neg\exists\, (M, V) \text{ within } O_t \text{ such that } V \vdash (M=F).

Proof. Suppose, for contradiction, that such $(M,V)$ exist. For $V$ to be a valid proof, the four necessary conditions for verification must simultaneously hold:

· (i) $\Pi$ invertible on the support (otherwise information is lost, contradicting exact reconstruction);
· (ii) the observer’s formal system $T$ can prove $\forall U, M(U)=F(U)$ — i.e., it can demonstrate complete behavioural equivalence;
· (iii) the observer can predict $F^{\,t}$ without simulating it step‑by‑step, enabling exhaustive testing of $M$ against all futures in finite time;
· (iv) the observer possesses unbounded storage and energy to house and execute the exact global model $M$ indefinitely.

We show each condition fails:

· (i) Contradicted by Theorem 4.1 (measurement bound).
· (ii) Contradicted by Theorem 4.2 (Gödel–Turing barrier).
· (iii) Contradicted by Theorem 4.4 (irreducibility precludes any predictive shortcut).
· (iv) Contradicted by Theorem 4.5 and Corollary 4.6 (thermodynamic resource bound).

Because at least one condition fails, the conjunction cannot be satisfied. $\square$

Corollary 5.3 (Observer‑Bounded Inference Principle).
Embedded finite observers with lossy observation channels cannot verifiably reconstruct the exact generative dynamics of computationally irreducible systems.

The principle is conditional: it applies only under the stated conjunction of irreducibility, lossy observation, and finite resources. It does not assert that the universe is unknowable, nor does it deny the possibility of an observer accidentally guessing $F$—only that it can never know it has done so.

