6. Algorithmic Information‑Theoretic Quantification: $\delta_t^*$

6.1 Robust Relative Compressibility Error

Let $K_C(x)$ denote the approximate Kolmogorov complexity of string $x$ estimated by a lossless compressor $C$. We use a set $\mathcal{C}=\{\text{LZMA},\;\text{bzip2},\;\text{Zstandard}\}$ of modern compressors, and define for a single compressor:
K_C(x) = |C(x)| \cdot 8 + \kappa, 


where $\kappa$ accounts for the fixed decompressor size (≈ 67 kB). For short strings, a minimum complexity floor $k_{\min}=128$ bits is enforced to avoid spurious ratios.

Conditional complexity is approximated via concatenation:
K_C(x \mid y) \approx K_C(y \Vert x) - K_C(y). 

Definition 6.1 (Robust relative compressibility error).
Given observation $X_t = \Pi(F(U_t))$ and serialised model weights $Y_t$ (as raw bytes), the error is
\delta_t^* = \sup_{C\in\mathcal{C}} \frac{K_C(X_t \mid Y_t)}{K_C(X_t)},


with the convention $\delta_t^*=1$ if the denominator is below $k_{\min}$.

This metric measures the fraction of algorithmic content in the observation that remains unexplained by the model. $\delta_t^* \approx 0$ if the model can losslessly compress the observations; $\delta_t^* \approx 1$ if it performs no better than random.

6.2 Error Decomposition

In parallel, we decompose the predictive loss $L_t$ (binary cross‑entropy) into:

· Epistemic error: $L_t - \bar{L}_t$, reducible by further learning.
· Aleatoric error: $\bar{L}_t$, an exponential moving average of $L_t$ that captures the noise floor.
· Irreducible residue: identified with $\bar{L}_t$ after convergence; this is the part attributable to computational irreducibility.

6.3 The Irreducible Error Floor Conjecture

Conjecture 6.1 (Positive irreducible error floor).
For any finite embedded observer in a computationally irreducible universe ($F\in\mathcal{I}$),
\liminf_{t\to\infty} \delta_t^* \ge \delta_{\min} > 0.


Conversely, for reducible systems ($F\notin\mathcal{I}$, e.g., Rule 90) or when the observation is invertible ($\Pi$ identity), $\delta_t^* \to 0$.

This conjecture is empirically testable via the in‑silico protocol described in Chapter 7 (of the full thesis). The scaling law $\delta_t^*(N) = \delta_\infty + A\cdot N^{-\alpha}$ can be fitted to data, with $\delta_\infty$ serving as an estimate of $\delta_{\min}$.

