7. Theoretical Grounding

· Algorithmic Information Dynamics (Abrahão et al. 2022): The act of observing is modelled as mutual perturbations between dynamical systems; our lossy channel $\Pi$ fits naturally into this picture. The robustness of emergent algorithmic information to the choice of compressor justifies the supremum in $\delta_t^*$.
· Observer as System Identification Algorithm (Khan 2025): Axiom 3.1 is a precise statement that the observer is an SIA with bounded Kolmogorov complexity.
· Subsystem Epistemics (Shi 2026): Our $\Pi_{\text{res}}$ matches the maximal subsystem resolution bound $r/2$.
· Good Regulator after Virgo et al. (2025): The theorem’s relaxation to partial homomorphic models explains why our impossibility does not forbid successful local regulation—only the pretence of global, verifiable exactness.

---

8. Conclusion

We have formalised the concept of an embedded observer in a deterministic, computationally irreducible universe and proved that, under the conjunction of measurement loss, logical incompleteness, computational irreducibility, and finite thermodynamic resources, verifiable exact reconstruction of the generative rule is impossible. The Observer‑Bounded Inference Principle captures this conditional limit. The introduction of the robust algorithmic metric $\delta_t^*$ turns a philosophical boundary into a measurable, empirically testable quantity. The framework thus opens a rigorous research programme on the structural limits of embedded science.

All theorems are conditional; they do not imply absolute unknowability, only that certifiable knowledge of the deepest generative rules is structurally precluded for any observer that is part of the system it studies.

