A.  Dynamical Systems and Computational Irreducibility

A.1  Deterministic Dynamical Systems

Definition A.1  A deterministic discrete‑time dynamical system is a pair (\mathcal{U},F) where \mathcal{U} is a set (the state space) and F:\mathcal{U}\to\mathcal{U} is a total function.  For an initial state U_0\in\mathcal{U} the orbit is given by

U_{t}=F^{\,t}(U_0),\qquad t\in\mathbb{N},
\]  

with F^{\,0}=\mathrm{id} and F^{\,t+1}=F\circ F^{\,t}.

In the main text we work with the class of elementary cellular automata (ECA) on finite lattices with periodic boundary conditions.  For a width‑W automaton, \mathcal{U}=\{0,1\}^{W} and F is induced by a local rule f:\{0,1\}^3\to\{0,1\} via

\bigl(F(s)\bigr)_i = f(s_{i-1},s_i,s_{i+1})\quad (\text{indices modulo }W).

A.2  Computational Irreducibility

Definition A.2  A total function F:\mathcal{U}\to\mathcal{U} is called computationally irreducible if there exists no computable function g:\mathbb{N}\to\mathcal{U} with g(t)=F^{\,t}(U_0) for all t that has a strictly lower worst‑case time complexity than simulating F step‑by‑step.  The class of all such generators is denoted by \mathcal{I}.

Lemma A.3  If F\in\mathcal{I} then any program that computes F^{\,t}(U_0) for all t requires \Omega(t) operations in the worst case.

Proof.  If a program with time complexity o(t) existed, it would violate the definition of irreducibility.  \square

Known members of \mathcal{I} include the elementary cellular automaton Rule 110 (Cook 2004) and very likely Rule 54 (Boccara et al. 2005).  In contrast, the additive rule Rule 90 (f(a,b,c)=a\oplus c) is reducible because F^{\,t}(s)_i = s_{i-t}\oplus s_{i+t} (mod W), which can be computed in O(\log t) time with fast exponentiation of binomial coefficients.

