C.  The Observation Channel and Information Loss

C.1  Decomposition of the Channel

The observation map \Pi is modelled as the composition of three operations:

1. Spatial coarse‑graining \Pi_{\mathrm{res}}: selects a contiguous patch of width w from the global state, i.e. (\Pi_{\mathrm{res}}(s))_i = s_{i+c} for i=0,\dots,w-1 (indices modulo W).
2. Physical noise \Pi_{\mathrm{phys}}: adds independent Gaussian noise with variance \sigma^2 to each pixel, followed by clipping to [0,1].
3. Computational bandwidth \Pi_{\mathrm{comp}}: the observer’s internal model has a finite hidden size and a bounded number of gradient updates per step; this constraint is not a function on the state but a limitation on the observer’s capacity to process information.

Because \Pi_{\mathrm{res}} and \Pi_{\mathrm{phys}} are non‑injective (many global states produce the same patch, and noise destroys fine detail), \Pi is strictly lossy.

C.2  Mutual Information Bound

Proposition C.1  Let U_t be the true state and X_t = \Pi(U_t) the observation.  For any channel \Pi that is not almost‑surely invertible,

I(X_t;U_t) < H(U_t).

Proof (classical case).  Write the stochastic map as a Markov kernel p(x|u).  The mutual information is

I(X;U) = H(U) - H(U\mid X) = H(U) - \sum_x p(x) H(U\mid X=x).
\]  

If there exist two distinct states u_1\neq u_2 with overlapping output distributions, then H(U\mid X=x) >0 for some x with positive probability, hence I(X;U) < H(U).  The quantum case (CPTP map) follows by writing a Stinespring dilation U\mapsto V(U\otimes|0\rangle\!\langle0|)V^\dagger and then tracing out the environment; the mutual information to the environment is strictly positive when the channel is non‑unitary.  \square

Theorem C.2 (Measurement limit)  The observation record \mathcal{O}_{\le t} cannot uniquely determine F.  There exist infinitely many distinct global dynamics F that are compatible with any finite lossy observation sequence.

Proof.  Because \Pi is non‑injective, pick two different initial states U_0^{(1)},U_0^{(2)} that yield the same observation sequence for all t (by selecting them such that their differences lie entirely outside the observed patch).  The evolution can differ arbitrarily in unobserved cells, leading to different F that agree on the observed data.  \square

