D.  Logical and Verification Constraint

D.1  Gödel and Turing Preliminaries

Theorem D.1 (Gödel 1931)  Any consistent formal system that contains elementary arithmetic is incomplete; there exist true statements that are unprovable within it.

Theorem D.2 (Turing 1936)  The halting problem for Turing machines is undecidable.

D.2  Proof of the Verification Barrier

Theorem D.3 (Gödel–Turing barrier)  Suppose the observer’s inference procedure is formalisable in a recursively axiomatisable theory T that extends Peano arithmetic.  If F is computationally universal (e.g., Rule 110), then there exists no proof in T that M=F for any candidate model M.

Proof.  Because F is Turing‑complete, there is a computable reduction \phi that maps any Turing machine \mathcal{M} and input x to an initial configuration U_0 of the cellular automaton such that the evolution of F from U_0 eventually produces a special marker symbol iff \mathcal{M}(x) halts.  Now assume, for contradiction, that the observer can prove M=F in T.  Then the observer can use M (which is a total computable function) to simulate the evolution from \phi(\mathcal{M},x) and decide whether the marker appears.  This yields a decision procedure for the halting problem: run the simulation using M and check for the marker.  Since the halting problem is undecidable, no such proof can exist.  \square

Corollary D.4  Even if the observer accidentally obtains a model that in fact equals F, it can never obtain a finite certificate of that identity.

---

E.  Thermodynamic Constraints

E.1  Landauer’s Principle

Theorem E.1 (Landauer 1961)  Erasing one bit of information in a memory at temperature T dissipates at least k_B T \ln 2 joules of heat into the environment.

E.2  Irreversibility in Neural Networks (Tkachenko 2025)

Proposition E.2  Linear operations in a neural network (matrix multiplications, convolutions) can, in principle, be performed with arbitrarily small thermodynamic cost if implemented reversibly.  By contrast, non‑linear activation functions such as ReLU and sigmoid are logically irreversible and necessarily erase information.  A sigmoid activation that squashes its input into [0,1] erases roughly half a bit of information per active neuron.

Proof sketch.  A sigmoid unit maps a continuous input to an output that is effectively a “decision” (e.g., >0.5 or <0.5).  The mapping is many‑to‑one, and by Landauer’s principle any such loss of information carries an energy cost.  The factor 0.5 is a heuristic average; the precise lower bound in the worst case is k_B T\ln 2 per bit.  \square

E.3  Effective Thermodynamic Cost Proxy

Definition E.3  In our experiments we model the cost as

E_{\mathrm{proxy}} = 0.5 \times (\text{number of active sigmoid neurons}) \times k_B T \ln 2,
\]  

where “active” means output > 0.5.  This is a physically motivated proxy; it is not claimed to be a strict lower bound for every architecture but captures the essential insight that non‑linear processing accumulates an irreversible energy bill.

Corollary E.4  An observer with a finite total energy budget E_{\max} cannot update an arbitrarily complex model over an indefinitely long lifetime; energetic constraints cap the total number of irreversible operations.

