F.  The Relative Compressibility Error \delta_t^*

F.1  Definition

Let X_t = \Pi(F(U_t))\in\mathbb{R}^{w} be the observed patch (converted to bytes) and Y_t the serialised model weights (flattened and concatenated in IEEE 754 float32).  For a compressor C, define

\delta_{t}^{C} = \frac{K_{\!C}(X_t\mid Y_t)}{K_{\!C}(X_t)},
\]  

with the convention \delta_{t}^{C}=0 if the denominator is below k_{\min}.  The robust error is the supremum:

\delta_t^* = \sup_{C\in\mathcal{C}} \delta_{t}^{C}.

F.2  Basic Properties

Lemma F.1  For any compressor C, 0\le \delta_{t}^{C}\le 1.

Proof.  K_{\!C}(X_t\mid Y_t)\ge 0 and, because K_{\!C}(X_t\mid Y_t)\le K_{\!C}(X_t) up to small additive constants (ignored due to the floor), the ratio is in [0,1].  \square

Lemma F.2  If the model M_t can losslessly predict the observation—i.e., the residual X_t can be compressed to zero length given Y_t—then \delta_t^*\to 0.  Conversely, if the model provides no compression benefit, \delta_t^* approaches 1.

F.3  Interpretation as an Irreducible Residue

For reducible systems (e.g., Rule 90) and noise‑free full observation, one expects that a sufficiently large observer can learn the exact rule, so \delta_t^* should vanish.  For irreducible systems, the observer’s internal model can never capture the full algorithmic structure of the observations, even asymptotically; hence a positive floor \delta_{\min}>0 should persist.

Conjecture F.3 (Positive Irreducible Error Floor)
If F\in\mathcal{I} and \Pi is lossy, then

\liminf_{t\to\infty} \delta_t^* \ge \delta_{\min} > 0.

This conjecture is empirally tested by the scaling law:

\delta_t^*(N) = \delta_\infty + A\cdot N^{-\alpha},
\]  

where N denotes the model capacity (hidden size).  The fitted parameter \delta_\infty estimates \delta_{\min}.

