G.  Full Proof of the Observer‑Bounded Inference Principle

We restate the main result with explicit hypotheses.

Theorem G.1 (Observer‑Bounded Inference — conditional impossibility)
Assume:

1. \mathcal{U} is an irreducible universe: F\in\mathcal{I} (Axiom 2.3).
2. The observer O_t is embedded (O_t\subset U_t), and its observation channel \Pi is non‑invertible (Definition 3.1, Proposition C.1).
3. The observer has finite computational and energetic resources (Axiom 3.1, §4.4).
4. The observer’s inference system is formalisable in a recursively axiomatisable theory containing elementary arithmetic.

Then the observer cannot achieve verifiable exact reconstruction of F. That is, there is no pair (M,V) such that V is a finite internal proof that the model M equals F on all states.

Proof.  Suppose, for contradiction, that such (M,V) exist.  For V to be a valid proof, the following four conditions must all be satisfied:

1. Full observability.  The observer must have gathered sufficient information to discriminate F from all other candidates.  This requires \Pi to be invertible on the relevant support, because otherwise infinitely many distinct F produce the same observations (Theorem C.2) – contradicting the fact that the observer has identified a unique true rule.
2. Verification completeness.  The formal system T must be able to prove the correctness of M, i.e., T\vdash\forall U\; M(U)=F(U).  By Theorem D.3, no such proof exists when F is Turing‑complete (Rule 110).  Hence condition (2) fails.
3. Prediction without simulation.  To exhaustively test that M agrees with F on all future states, the observer must be able to outrun the universe’s step‑by‑step evolution, because the number of possible futures is infinite.  By Lemma A.3, computational irreducibility forbids any shortcut; the observer, being embedded, cannot compute F^{\,t} faster than the universe itself.  Thus exhaustive verification is impossible within any finite time.
4. Unbounded physical resources.  Maintaining and updating an exact, globally consistent model M=F over indefinite time would demand memory and energy that exceed any finite budget.  The thermodynamic cost of irreversible operations (Proposition E.2) grows with the lifetime and complexity of the model.  Corollary E.4 tells us that a finite energy budget imposes a cap on the total number of irreversible updates; an exact model of an irreducible system would require an unbounded number of them.

Since at least one of these necessary conditions is violated, the conjunction cannot be satisfied.  Therefore no verifiable exact reconstruction exists.  \square

Corollary G.2 (Observer‑Bounded Inference Principle).
Embedded finite observers with lossy observation channels cannot verifiably reconstruct the exact generative dynamics of computationally irreducible systems.

---

H.  The Halting Classifier H_O and Scaling Laws

H.1  Three‑Valued Halting Classifier

Let \{\delta_t\} be the sequence of robust errors.  For a window w=100 and trend length \tau=50, the classifier outputs:

H_O(t)=
\begin{cases}
\mathrm{Stable} & \text{if }\ \operatorname{std}( \delta_{t-\tau+1},\dots,\delta_t ) < \varepsilon,\\[4pt]
\mathrm{Diverging} & \text{if } \bar{\delta}_{\text{recent}} - \bar{\delta}_{\text{older}} > \varepsilon,\\[4pt]
\mathrm{Undecided} & \text{otherwise},
\end{cases}
\]  

with \varepsilon=0.02.  The stability condition is motivated by martingale convergence: if \delta_t converges to a limit, it eventually behaves like a supermartingale with shrinking increments, leading to low variance.

H.2  Scaling Law and Power‑Law Fitting

To separate capacity‑dependent reducible error from the structural irreducible floor, we use the model

\delta_t^*(N) = \delta_{\infty} + A \cdot N^{-\alpha},
\]  

where N is the number of hidden units (a proxy for model capacity).  The parameters (\delta_{\infty},A,\alpha) are estimated by nonlinear least‑squares via scipy.optimize.curve_fit.  For reducible systems we expect \delta_{\infty}\approx 0; for irreducible ones, \delta_{\infty}>0 (with statistical significance).  This provides the empirical grounding for Conjecture F.3.

---

I.  Connections to Existing Formalisms

Algorithmic Information Dynamics (Abrahão et al. 2022) formalises observation as mutual perturbation; our \delta_t^* inherits the robustness to the choice of reference machine.
System Identification Algorithm theory (Khan 2025) justifies Axiom 3.1: the observer’s model complexity is bounded by its own Kolmogorov complexity.
Subsystem Epistemics (Shi 2026) provides the resolution bound that underpins \Pi_{\mathrm{res}}.
The Good Regulator theorem (Virgo et al. 2025) explains why regulation remains possible even though exact reconstruction is impossible.

